  "prompt": "[system]\n\n    You generate compact, robust Python TSP metaheuristics.\n    You must follow the requested interface exactly, always preserve tour validity.\n    \n\n[user]\nPARENTS:\nCandidate cand_000005: random_greedy_2opt\n\nStatus: valid; distance: 32.8364\n\nThought: Construct initial tour using randomized greedy that selects from nearest 3 unvisited cities. Then apply 2-opt improvement until convergence or budget exhausted.\n\nHistory source: yes\nHistory bucket: tsp:size:500\nHistory role: regime specialist\n\nCode:\n\n```python\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport random\n\ndef solve_tsp(distance_matrix: np.ndarray, seed: int, budget: int) -> np.ndarray:\n    random.seed(seed)\n    n = distance_matrix.shape[0]\n    if n <= 2:\n        tour = np.arange(n, dtype=np.int32)\n        return tour\n    # Randomized greedy construction\n    unvisited = set(range(n))\n    start = random.randrange(n)\n    tour = [start]\n    unvisited.remove(start)\n    while unvisited:\n        current = tour[-1]\n        # Get distances to unvisited\n        cand = list(unvisited)\n        dists = [(distance_matrix[current, c], c) for c in cand]\n        dists.sort()\n        # Choose among the 3 nearest (or less if fewer)\n        k = min(3, len(dists))\n        choice = random.randrange(k)\n        next_city = dists[choice][1]\n        tour.append(next_city)\n        unvisited.remove(next_city)\n    tour = np.array(tour, dtype=np.int32)\n    best_tour = tour.copy()\n    best_dist = sum(distance_matrix[tour[i], tour[(i+1)%n]] for i in range(n))\n    # 2-opt improvement\n    iteration = 0\n    improved = True\n    while improved and iteration < budget:\n        improved = False\n        for i in range(n-2):\n            for j in range(i+2, n):\n                iteration += 1\n                if iteration >= budget:\n                    break\n                # Check if reversing segment (i+1..j) reduces distance\n                a, b, c, d = tour[i], tour[i+1], tour[j], tour[(j+1)%n]\n                delta = distance_matrix[a, c] + distance_matrix[b, d] - distance_matrix[a, b] - distance_matrix[c, d]\n                if delta < -1e-12:\n                    # Apply 2-opt move\n                    tour[i+1:j+1] = tour[i+1:j+1][::-1]\n                    best_tour = tour.copy()\n                    # Update best_dist (not necessary but for potential use)\n                    best_dist = best_dist + delta\n                    improved = True\n                    report_best_tour(best_tour)\n                    break\n            if improved or iteration >= budget:\n                break\n    return best_tour\n\n```\n\nVERBAL GRADIENT: follow the reflection below as the primary source of change.\n\nUse the supplied parents as supporting evidence only.\n\nPARENT-SPECIFIC LLM REFLECTIONS:\n\nParent cand_000005 LLM reflection:\n- Summary: Randomized greedy + 2-opt achieved good 500-city results; mean distance 32.84 with fast runtime and no invalid tours.\n- Aim: Improve construction heuristic to produce higher-quality starting tours, reducing tour length without exceeding budget.\n- Preserve: budget-aware 2-opt loop; valid permutation guarantee; report_best_tour mechanism; fast runtime profile\n- Change: replace randomized greedy with nearest insertion or regret-based construction; add a simple perturbation restart if time allows within budget\n- Avoid: introducing invalid tours; increasing runtime beyond budget tolerance; unnecessary complexity that does not reduce tour length; breaking the 2-opt core correctness\n\nDistance is the search objective for TSP; lower distance is better.\n\nImplement this function only:\n\ndef solve_tsp(distance_matrix: np.ndarray, seed: int, budget: int) -> np.ndarray:\n\nRequirements:\n- Minimize total TSP tour distance.\n- Return a 1D np.ndarray containing each node exactly once.\n- Do not repeat the start node at the end.\n- Always return a valid tour, even when budget is very small.\n- Use only distance_matrix